09-04-2026 Module 7 Exception Handling and Error Management

Exception
    - An event that occurs during execution that disrupts the normal flow
    - Checked - checked at compile time, must be handled or declared
    - Unchecked - checked at runtime, not mandatory to handle, (NullPointerException, Arithmetic Exception)
    - Errors - Beyond application control, (OOM, StackOverflow), usually cannot recover from

    - Prevent pipeline termination due to bad data
    - Handle close to its source, never with an empty catch block

Throw
    - Throw meaningful exceptions using throw keyword


Checked Exceptions
    - Verified at Compile Time
    - Caught or Declared
    - catch, error.getMessage();
    - throws IOException (non customizable)

Unchecked Exceptions
    - subclasses of RuntimeException
    - occur at runtime
    - mostly logic bugs
    - eg NullPointerException, ArithmeticException, ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException

finally
    - need, to validate that the code beforehand ran
    - used for cleanup
    - if an exception occurs in finally, it can override a previous exception


try-with-resources
    - Automatically closes resources that implement AutoCloseable (File, Steam, Connection), at end of the try block
    - vs finally, AutoCloseable resource (file, socket, DB connection) use try-with,
        - Non-resource cleanup (resetting state, clearing a flag) -> use finally


throw vs throws
    - throw - used to explicitly throw an exception from a method or block
        - creates an exception object and throws it
    - throws - used in method signature to declare that method may throw one or more exception
        - It does not throw the exception, it passes the responsibility to the caller (eg IOException)
    - throws DECLARE IT (it may happen), throw DOES IT

Exception propagation
    - Exception propagates up method chain, if not caught in other methods passes all the way back up to main
    - If no method catches it, the JVM handles it

Error Handling Strategies
    - Log and escalate when needed, put end user first with meaningful messages
    - Recover or fail safely
    - Detect error and then handle gracefully
    - Be specific with errors

    - Retry transient failures
    - Fallback / Default
    - Skip and Continue
    - Fail Fast
    - Graceful Degradation (reduce functionality but keep system available)
    - Circuit Breaker

Logging
    - Any event failed or caught exception, should be logged
    - SLF4J, import org.slf4j.logger and import org.slf4j.loggerFactory
    - store in JSON format for clarity
    - log destinations, eg AWS
